\description{
\code{lsa()} fits a lag sequential analysis model to event sequences, wide
sequence tables, transition-count matrices, long event logs, or sequence
objects from other packages. Lag sequential analysis (Sackett, 1979; Bakeman
& Gottman, 1997) tests whether, in a stream of coded events, one state
follows another more or less often than chance predicts.

The transitions observed at a given lag are counted into a table of
antecedent (\code{from}) by consequent (\code{to}) states; an expected table is derived
under the hypothesis that the two are independent; and each cell is tested
with an adjusted residual (Haberman, 1979), a standard normal deviate that is
positive when the transition is over-represented and negative when it is
avoided. The significant cells define a lag transition network, and the
tablewise likelihood-ratio and Pearson tests show whether there is any
sequential dependence at all. For every cell, \code{lsa()} estimates the observed
and expected counts, the transition probability, the adjusted residual and
its p-value, Yule's Q, kappa and lift. The \code{engine} selects the test: the
classical analysis of Bakeman and Quera (1995) by default; a two-cell (2 x 2)
test with odds ratios; Sackett's bidirectional test on the symmetrised table;
or the parallel and non-parallel dominance tests of Sackett (1979) and
Wampold (1984). Self-transitions or any other cells that cannot occur by
construction are declared as structural zeros, and the expected table is then
fitted by iterative proportional fitting (Christensen, 1997). With \code{group},
one model is fitted per group on a shared state set so the groups can be
compared.

It returns an \code{lsa} object, or with \code{group} an \code{lsa_group} object holding one
fit per group.

The fit is read with \code{\link[=transitions]{transitions()}}, \code{\link[=nodes]{nodes()}}, \code{\link[=tests]{tests()}}, \code{\link[=initial]{initial()}}
and \code{\link[=transition_probabilities]{transition_probabilities()}}, and drawn with \code{\link[=plot.lsa]{plot.lsa()}}. To fit
several lags at once use \code{\link[=lsa_lags]{lsa_lags()}}; to assess the transitions use
\code{\link[=bootstrap_lsa]{bootstrap_lsa()}}, \code{\link[=certainty_lsa]{certainty_lsa()}} or \code{\link[=permute_lsa]{permute_lsa()}}; to compare
groups use \code{\link[=compare_lsa]{compare_lsa()}}. The engine wrappers \code{lsa_classical()},
\code{lsa_two_cell()}, \code{lsa_bidirectional()}, \code{lsa_parallel_dominance()} and
\code{lsa_nonparallel_dominance()} are \code{lsa()} with \code{engine} fixed and return
the same object.
}
\section{Long-format input}{

A long event log is turned into sequences before the model is fitted:
one sequence per \code{actor}, per \code{session}, or per actor-session pair, or a
single sequence when neither is given. Events are ordered within a
sequence by \code{order}, or else by \code{time}; when \code{time} is given without
\code{session}, a gap longer than \code{time_threshold} seconds starts a new
sequence. If the session identifier is already unique across actors,
pass it as \code{actor} and leave \code{session} unset. Check the number of
sequences reported by \code{summary(fit)} against the number expected.
}

\section{Conditions}{

Errors carry the class \code{lagdynamics_error} and one of:
\itemize{
\item \code{lagdynamics_bad_input}: invalid data, labels, group, structural-zero
matrix or long-format column.
\item \code{lagdynamics_unknown_engine}: \code{engine} is not registered.
\item \code{lagdynamics_missing_package}: the engine needs a package that is not
installed.
\item \code{lagdynamics_no_transitions}: the data contain no transition at the
requested lag.
\item \code{lagdynamics_unsupported_option}: the engine does not support
structural zeros.
\item \code{lagdynamics_ipf_infeasible}: the structural zeros leave a state with
observed transitions but no permitted cell.
\item \code{lagdynamics_needs_events}: \code{group} was given with a
transition-count matrix, which cannot be split.
}

With structural zeros (\code{loops = FALSE} or \code{structural_zeros}), the
expected counts, adjusted residuals and the tablewise G^2 come from the
quasi-independence model fitted to the possible cells (Bakeman & Quera,
1995, \emph{Psychological Bulletin}): its margins are the totals of the
possible cells, so transitions that the data contain in a forbidden cell
are left out of the fit. The observed counts and transition probabilities
still report them. A warning of class \code{lagdynamics_structural_zero_counts}
is raised when this happens, naming the cells, because the data
contradict the declaration.
}

